WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:20.440
So, I'm here to share my photography.

00:20.440 --> 00:22.880
Or is it photography?

00:22.880 --> 00:27.720
Because of course, this is a photograph that you can't take with your camera.

00:27.720 --> 00:34.040
My interest in photography started as I got my first digital camera at the age of 15.

00:34.040 --> 00:38.900
It mixed with my earlier passion for drawing, but it was a bit different.

00:38.900 --> 00:42.740
Because using the camera, the process was in the planning instead.

00:42.740 --> 00:48.080
And when you take a photograph with the camera, it's just the process ends when you press

00:48.080 --> 00:49.760
the trigger.

00:49.760 --> 00:53.960
So to me it felt like photography was more about being at the right place at the right

00:53.960 --> 00:54.960
time.

00:55.520 --> 00:58.500
I felt like anyone could do that.

00:58.500 --> 01:03.560
So I wanted to create something different, something where the process starts when you

01:03.560 --> 01:06.140
press the trigger.

01:06.140 --> 01:13.300
Photos like this, construction going on along a busy road, but it has an unexpected twist.

01:13.300 --> 01:17.800
And despite that, it retains a level of realism.

01:18.480 --> 01:25.880
Photos like these, both dark and colorful, but all with a common goal of retaining the

01:25.880 --> 01:28.520
level of realism.

01:28.520 --> 01:36.040
When I say realism, I mean photorealism, because of course, it's not something you can capture

01:36.040 --> 01:41.920
really, but I always wanted to look like it could have been captured somehow as a photograph.

01:41.920 --> 01:47.000
Photos where you will need a brief moment to think, to figure out the trick.

01:47.000 --> 01:52.680
So it's more about capturing an idea than about capturing a moment really.

01:52.680 --> 01:56.880
But what's the trick that makes it look realistic?

01:56.880 --> 02:00.280
Is it something about the details or the colors?

02:00.280 --> 02:03.260
Is it something about the light?

02:03.260 --> 02:07.400
What creates the illusion?

02:07.400 --> 02:12.440
Sometimes the perspective is the illusion, but in the end it comes down to how we interpret

02:12.440 --> 02:16.120
the world and how it can be realized on a two-dimensional surface.

02:17.000 --> 02:22.760
It's not really what is realistic, it's what we think looks realistic really.

02:22.760 --> 02:26.320
So I think the basics are quite simple.

02:26.320 --> 02:30.940
I just see it as a puzzle of reality, where you just can take different pieces of reality

02:30.940 --> 02:35.240
and put it together to create alternate reality.

02:35.240 --> 02:38.480
Let me show you a simple example.

02:38.480 --> 02:42.340
Here we have three perfectly imaginable physical objects.

02:42.340 --> 02:45.800
Something we all can relate to living in a three-dimensional world.

02:45.800 --> 02:52.120
But combined in a certain way, they can create something that still looks three-dimensional.

02:52.120 --> 02:54.480
Like it could exist.

02:54.480 --> 02:57.280
But at the same time we know it can't.

02:57.280 --> 03:01.560
So we trick our brains because our brain simply doesn't accept the fact that it doesn't really

03:01.560 --> 03:03.480
make sense.

03:03.480 --> 03:08.520
And I see the same process with combining photographs.

03:08.520 --> 03:12.840
It's just really about combining different realities.

03:12.840 --> 03:17.040
So the things that make a photograph look realistic.

03:17.040 --> 03:19.400
I think it's the things that we don't even think about.

03:19.400 --> 03:24.120
The things all around us in our daily lives.

03:24.120 --> 03:28.220
But when combining photographs, this is really important to consider.

03:28.220 --> 03:32.120
Because otherwise it just looks wrong somehow.

03:32.120 --> 03:36.200
So I would like to say that there are three simple rules to follow to achieve a realistic

03:36.200 --> 03:38.720
result.

03:38.720 --> 03:41.300
As you can see these images aren't really special.

03:41.300 --> 03:47.660
But combined they can create something like this.

03:47.660 --> 03:52.180
So the first rule is that photos combined should have the same perspective.

03:52.180 --> 03:57.460
Secondly, photos combined should have the same type of light.

03:57.460 --> 04:00.300
And these two images both fulfill these two requirements.

04:00.300 --> 04:04.300
Shot at the same height and in the same type of light.

04:04.300 --> 04:08.740
The third one is about making it impossible to distinguish where the different images

04:08.740 --> 04:10.520
begin and end.

04:10.520 --> 04:12.920
By making it seamless.

04:12.920 --> 04:17.440
Make it impossible to say how the image actually was composed.

04:17.440 --> 04:23.440
So by matching color, contrast and brightness in the borders between the different images.

04:23.440 --> 04:30.360
Adding photographic defects like depth of field, desaturated colors and noise.

04:30.360 --> 04:35.280
Erase the borders between the different images and make it look like one single image.

04:35.280 --> 04:40.720
Despite the fact that one image can contain hundreds of layers basically.

04:40.720 --> 04:46.280
So here's another example.

04:46.280 --> 04:50.120
One might think that this is just an image of a landscape and the lower part is what's

04:50.120 --> 04:51.760
manipulated.

04:51.760 --> 04:57.800
But this image is actually entirely composed of photographs from different locations.

04:57.800 --> 05:02.960
I personally think that it's easier to actually create a place than to find a place.

05:02.960 --> 05:06.960
Because then you don't need to compromise with the ideas in your head.

05:06.960 --> 05:10.600
But it does require a lot of planning.

05:10.600 --> 05:13.840
And getting this idea during winter, I knew that I had several months to plan it.

05:13.840 --> 05:18.960
To find the different locations for the pieces for the puzzle basically.

05:18.960 --> 05:23.640
So for example the fish is captured on a fishing trip.

05:23.640 --> 05:25.520
The shores are from a different location.

05:25.520 --> 05:28.760
The underwater part is captured in a stone pit.

05:28.760 --> 05:34.680
And I even turned the house on top of the island red to make it look more Swedish.

05:34.680 --> 05:39.840
So to achieve a realistic result, I think it comes down to planning.

05:39.840 --> 05:43.880
It always starts with a sketch, an idea.

05:43.880 --> 05:47.400
Then it's about combining the different photographs.

05:47.400 --> 05:50.280
And here every piece is very well planned.

05:50.280 --> 05:55.720
And if you do a good job capturing the photos, the result can be quite beautiful.

05:55.720 --> 05:58.800
And also quite realistic.

05:58.800 --> 06:02.800
So all the tools are out there.

06:02.800 --> 06:10.760
And the only thing that limits us is our imagination.

06:10.760 --> 06:11.760
Thank you.

06:11.760 --> 06:12.760
Thank you.

06:12.760 --> 06:12.780
Thank you.

